  "query_preview": "..An upper low centered near Las Vegas early this\nmorning will lift northeastward through the day, leaving behind\nsome weak troughing through southern California down to Baja.\nInstability ahead of the trough is producing scattered showers and\nthunderstorms from NW Arizona into Utah this morning but these are\nlikely to mostly end by mid morning. A disturbance over northeast\nSonora early this morning is forecast to drift northward along the\nAZ/NM state line today through Wednesday. Deep moisture will\naccompany this feature, with precipitable water values forecast to\nrise dramatically today in eastern Arizona, spreading westward\nWednesday to include Yavapai/Coconino counties as well. Latest\nensemble forecasts show PW reaching the 95th+ percentile for late\nSeptember over the eastern third of the state during this period. \nShower and thunderstorm chances will be highest in Apache and\nNavajo counties this afternoon through tonight, with a few pockets\nof heavy rainfall/flash flooding. By Wednesday - highest rain\nchances remain over Apache County, but as the moisture spreads\nwest, additional scattered thunderstorms are forecast as far west\nas Flagstaff and Prescott. Some of these may also produce locally\nheavy rainfall, and WPC has placed nearly all of our forecast area\nin a marginal risk for excessive rainfall/flash flooding. On\nThursday - the weak trough off the southern California/Baja coast\nis forecast to lift northeastward across our region. This should\nprovide additional forcing for another round of showers and\nthunderstorms over much of central/northern Arizona with locally\nheavy rainfall again the main threat.\n\nFriday through the weekend - most of the guidance indicates drier\nconditions Friday and especially Saturday as the disturbances move\nnortheast of the state and drier southwest flow develops. We'll\nneed to monitor the possibility of deeper moisture getting pulled\nnorthward from a couple of tropical systems well to the south\ntoward Sunday/Monday as a trough develops off the west coast. For\nnow, PoP values move back upward to 20-40% by this time period.",
